      <p>The train journey from Kandy to Ella is consistently ranked among the world's most beautiful railway routes — winding for six hours through tea-carpeted mountains, over colonial-era viaducts, past waterfalls and misty cloud forest. But Sri Lanka's rail network offers much more than one famous stretch. This guide covers every major route, how to book seats (including the coveted observation car), class differences, timetables and everything else you need to travel Sri Lanka's railways with confidence.</p>

      <h2>Why Take the Train in Sri Lanka?</h2>
      <p>Sri Lanka's rail network is slow, sometimes unpunctual, occasionally crowded — and absolutely worth it. The highland mainline from Colombo through Kandy to Badulla cuts through some of the most dramatic scenery in Asia, climbing from sea-level coastal plains to the cool mountains of the Hill Country. Unlike the roads, which twist endlessly and can turn a 100km journey into three exhausting hours, the train tracks follow river valleys and ridgelines that offer continuously changing panoramas. It is also cheap — a reserved second-class seat from Kandy to Ella costs less than USD 5. The experience itself is the point: chai vendors working through the carriages, schoolchildren in white uniforms, monks in saffron, small stations whose names read like a poem.</p>

      <h2>The Sri Lanka Rail Network: Overview</h2>
      <p>Sri Lanka Railways (SLR) operates a network of about 1,500km of metre-gauge track, centred on Colombo Fort station. Five main lines radiate from Colombo:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Main Line</strong> — Colombo to Badulla via Kandy, Nanu Oya (Nuwara Eliya) and Ella. The most scenic route.</li>
        <li><strong>Coastal Line</strong> — Colombo to Matara along the south coast, passing Aluthgama, Hikkaduwa, Galle and Weligama.</li>
        <li><strong>Northern Line</strong> — Colombo to Jaffna via Anuradhapura. Restored after the war, now a full cross-island route.</li>
        <li><strong>Puttalam Line</strong> — Colombo to Puttalam up the northwest coast, useful for Negombo and Kalpitiya.</li>
        <li><strong>Kelani Valley Line</strong> — Colombo to Avissawella, a narrow suburban line with character.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>For tourists, the Main Line (especially the Kandy–Ella section) and the Coastal Line are the most useful. The Northern Line to Jaffna is worth considering if you're heading north.</p>

      <h2>The Highland Route: Colombo to Badulla</h2>
      <p>This is the one. The full journey from Colombo to Badulla takes roughly nine hours; most tourists join at Kandy (3.5 hours from Colombo) or Peradeniya Junction, and ride to Ella (about 6 hours from Kandy) or Badulla. The train climbs steadily from the hot lowlands, entering tea country above Hatton. From Nanu Oya onwards — the station for Nuwara Eliya — the scenery becomes extraordinary: rolling hills entirely covered in manicured green tea bushes, mist drifting through valleys, workers in bright saris carrying baskets of leaves. The Demodara Loop near Ella — where the track curves through a 360° spiral to gain elevation — is a feat of Victorian engineering that the train passes through without announcement, as if it's nothing special.</p>

      <h3>Key Stations on the Main Line</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Colombo Fort</strong> — main terminus, all long-distance trains start here</li>
        <li><strong>Peradeniya Junction</strong> — for Kandy (change here or alight at Kandy station)</li>
        <li><strong>Kandy</strong> — spur from Peradeniya, 1km from town centre</li>
        <li><strong>Hatton</strong> — gateway to Adam's Peak and tea country</li>
        <li><strong>Nanu Oya</strong> — the station for Nuwara Eliya (9km away by bus or tuk-tuk)</li>
        <li><strong>Haputale</strong> — a ridge-top town with extraordinary views, underrated</li>
        <li><strong>Ella</strong> — the most popular stop; vibrant backpacker town with great hikes</li>
        <li><strong>Badulla</strong> — end of the line, quieter town, less visited</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Ticket Classes Explained</h2>
      <p>Sri Lanka trains have three classes — and understanding the difference will significantly affect your experience.</p>

      <h3>Third Class (Unreserved)</h3>
      <p>Wooden benches, no reservations, can become very crowded. Fine for short journeys (under an hour). Not recommended for the Kandy–Ella stretch unless you're on an extremely tight budget — standing for six hours through winding mountain track is not comfortable.</p>

      <h3>Second Class (Reserved)</h3>
      <p>Padded seats, ceiling fans or AC, reserved seats. This is the sweet spot. Book a window seat on the right side when travelling Kandy→Ella, or the left side when going Ella→Kandy, for the best views (or just try both sides as scenery appears on both). Cost: approximately LKR 400–600 (USD 1.30–2) for the Kandy–Ella journey.</p>

      <h3>First Class (AC)</h3>
      <p>Air-conditioned, wider seats, quieter carriages. The comfort improvement over second class is modest and the sealed windows distance you from the experience — the tea-scented mountain air, the vendors at stations, the ability to lean out a window and take photographs. Most experienced travellers prefer second class for the highland route. Cost: approximately LKR 1,000–1,500 (USD 3–5) for Kandy–Ella.</p>

      <h3>Observation Saloon Car</h3>
      <p>The Expo Rail observation cars are private-operator carriages attached to certain Sri Lanka Railways trains. They have panoramic windows, forward-facing seats and a viewing platform. Enormously popular with tourists — they routinely sell out weeks in advance during peak season (December–March). The experience is genuinely excellent: the windows allow video and photography without obstruction, the carriages are comfortable, and the forward view from the observation platform is unmatched. Cost: approximately LKR 2,000–2,500 (USD 6–8) per person. Book at <strong>exporail.lk</strong> — the official Expo Rail website — as early as possible.</p>

