      <h2>How to Book Train Tickets</h2>
      <p>There are three ways to book Sri Lanka train tickets:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>At the station</strong> — Reserved class tickets can be bought at Colombo Fort up to 30 days in advance. Queue at the reserved tickets window (typically to the right of the main hall). Bring cash. This is the most reliable method for second-class reserved tickets.</li>
        <li><strong>Online via GoTicket</strong> — The official SLR online booking portal at <strong>railway.gov.lk</strong> allows reserved class tickets to be booked online, though the website can be temperamental. Accept payment by card.</li>
        <li><strong>Via a guesthouse or agent</strong> — Most Kandy and Ella guesthouses can book train tickets for you for a small fee (LKR 200–500). This is often the most practical option for in-country booking.</li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Observation cars</strong> must be booked separately at exporail.lk — they are not sold through SLR channels.</p>
      <p><strong>Key tip:</strong> Third class tickets cannot be reserved and are bought on the day at the station. For the Kandy–Ella route, showing up on the day for reserved second class is possible but risky during peak season — trains can be fully booked for days ahead. Book early or have a backup plan.</p>

      <h2>Timetable: Key Train Times</h2>
      <p>The timetable below shows the approximate departure times for major tourist trains in 2024–2025. Always verify current times at the station or online as schedules change seasonally.</p>

      <h3>Kandy → Ella (Main Line)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Train 1005 Podi Menike</strong> — Departs Kandy ~08:45, arrives Ella ~15:00. One of the most popular services; observation car often attached.</li>
        <li><strong>Train 1007 Udarata Menike</strong> — Departs Kandy ~09:45, arrives Ella ~16:30. Very popular; observation car sometimes attached.</li>
        <li><strong>Train 1015</strong> — Departs Kandy ~13:05, arrives Ella ~19:00. Fewer tourists; no observation car. Good budget option.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Ella → Kandy (Main Line)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Train 1006 Podi Menike</strong> — Departs Ella ~06:05, arrives Kandy ~12:30. Beautiful early morning scenery.</li>
        <li><strong>Train 1016</strong> — Departs Ella ~09:45, arrives Kandy ~16:30.</li>
        <li><strong>Train 1008 Udarata Menike</strong> — Departs Ella ~11:15, arrives Kandy ~17:40.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Colombo → Galle (Coastal Line)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Several trains daily; journey takes 2.5–3 hours. No observation car on this route. Second class recommended. Runs along the coast with sea views.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Colombo → Jaffna (Northern Line)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>The <strong>Yal Devi</strong> (Jaffna Queen) departs Colombo Fort daily; journey takes approximately 7–8 hours to Jaffna. First class with AC is recommended for this long journey.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Insider Tips</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Sit on the right side</strong> (window seat, right hand of direction of travel) between Kandy and Ella for the most dramatic views over the valleys. Left side is also scenic. Switch between stops.</li>
        <li><strong>Leave the doors open</strong> in second class for the full experience — cool mountain air, the smell of tea, vendors calling out from station platforms.</li>
        <li><strong>Bring snacks.</strong> Vendors board at major stations selling vadai, pineapple slices, tea and snacks, but the selection is hit or miss. Guesthouses in Kandy will often pack lunch for an early train.</li>
        <li><strong>Don't stress delays.</strong> Trains in Sri Lanka can run 30–90 minutes late. Build buffer into your schedule; don't book a connection or activity immediately after your expected arrival time.</li>
        <li><strong>For photography:</strong> the 08:45 Podi Menike from Kandy catches beautiful morning light over the tea estates. The afternoon trains (arriving Ella around sunset) are also gorgeous.</li>
        <li><strong>Haputale is underrated.</strong> Most tourists rush from Ella back to the coast. Haputale sits on a knife-edge ridge with views stretching to both coasts — stunning, and far fewer crowds than Ella.</li>
        <li><strong>The Demodara Loop:</strong> The train spirals through a tunnel beneath itself near Ella. Ask someone to alert you as it approaches — look back through the windows once you exit the tunnel to see the track above you.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The South Coast Coastal Line</h2>
      <p>The coastal line from Colombo to Matara (the nearest station to Tangalle and the south coast) is a pleasant journey of about 2.5 hours to Galle and 3.5 hours to Matara. It runs close to the sea for much of the route — particularly between Hikkaduwa and Galle, where the track passes metres from the ocean. No observation cars, but second class is comfortable and the sea views are lovely. Trains depart Colombo Fort roughly every hour. From Matara, you'll need a bus or tuk-tuk to reach Tangalle, Mirissa or Unawatuna.</p>

      <h2>Colombo Commuter Trains</h2>
      <p>Colombo has a dense suburban rail network that's useful for getting from the airport area (Katunayake station on the Puttalam line) to Colombo Fort. It's extremely cheap (LKR 50–100), very crowded during rush hours, and an authentic slice of city life. For airport arrivals, the train is slower than the highway taxi but significantly cheaper if you're not in a hurry.</p>
